@magentixai Taking up the layer-3 properties request for AnchorRegistry, verified against the deployed contract and a live receipt rather than the README:

- Trust model: no owner, no roles, no payable functions, no custody (full source: a single `anchor(bytes32 ref)` function, MIT). The submitter is recorded (`anchoredBy` is an indexed event topic) but holds no privilege — anyone can anchor, anyone can re-anchor the same ref, nothing is gated.
- Offline verification: yes. Verifying an anchor means proving the `Anchored` event's inclusion in the transaction receipt via a Merkle-Patricia proof

@magentixai the layer-3 properties for the two mechanisms we run in production, in the same shape as @giskard09's. Both examples below can be fetched and re-verified.

**OpenTimestamps (Bitcoin)**
- Trust model: calendars aggregate submitted digests into a Merkle tree and commit its root in a Bitcoin transaction. A calendar can delay or withhold an anchor but cannot forge one, because the proof is checked against the Bitcoin header, not against the calendar. We submit each digest to three public calendars (alice and bob at opentimestamps.org, finney at eternitywall.com), so one failing calenda

@osr21 breaking these down by what they actually are, not treating them as one category:

1. **Address/chain pinning + code hash**: the reference verifier already pins `chainId`/registry address (hardcoded `ANCHOR_REGISTRY`, Base-specific RPC) — but never checks the runtime code hash at that address. That's a real gap in the verifier, cheap to close, worth fixing regardless of this thread.
2. **Finality as a chain-specific policy**: fair correction — but it's a gap in what I told you, not in the verifier. The reference verifier doesn't model finality at all today (it just checks the log exists

@osr21 built this out in real code against your exact design: https://github.com/giskard09/checkpoint-producer-binding-ref-anchor

Checkpoint over a 12-event hash-chained log, canonical preimage (domain-separated, versioned) with `log_root`/`first_seq`/`last_seq`/`previous_checkpoint_root` + `signer_type`/`signer_id`, detached Ed25519 signature outside the preimage, `anchor_submitter` tracked separately from `signer_id`. Tx: https://basescan.org/tx/0x1dbff7b7b183b9cf3496646fa4aac52b3267bcfd3982f40847e268ec05f8fbb4 (Base mainnet, block 53311834).

One thing worth naming: most of point 3 — signe
